-- =============================================================================================
-- MJ Forms v0.8.x — the metadata seed
-- =============================================================================================
-- Generated by `mj sync push --dir metadata` against a database whose Forms
-- metadata had been emptied first, so every statement below is a CREATE and the file is
-- replayable on a fresh install. 82 records: 2 roles, 15 entity permissions, 6 actions
-- (+9 params), 1 action category, 2 AI prompts (+2 prompt models), 1 prompt category, 2 templates
-- (+2 contents, +5 params), the Forms application (+7 application entities, +2 application roles),
-- 2 dashboards, 1 user view, 11 form styles, 9 form categories, and the automation service
-- principal with its role grant.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST. `mj-app.json`'s `metadata.directory` is documentation and nothing
-- else — MJ's manifest schema says so in as many words, and the install engine never reads it.
-- Seeding happens exclusively through `migrations/`. Until this file, MJ Forms shipped no seed at
-- all: a clean `mj app install` produced an install with no Form Respondent role, no CanCreate
-- grant on the response entities, no styles, no categories, no app or nav, no dashboards and no AI
-- authoring — which is to say the anonymous submit path, the product, could not run. Every step
-- reported success. See plans/DISTRIBUTION_SEED_PLAN.md.
--
-- TWO SUBSTITUTIONS WERE APPLIED TO THE GENERATOR'S OUTPUT, and both are load-bearing:
--
--   1. `${flyway:defaultSchema}` -> `${mjSchema}` on all 67 core stored-procedure calls.
--      MetadataSync writes core SP calls with the DEFAULT-schema placeholder because in MJ's own
--      repo the default schema IS the core schema. Here it is `__mj_BizAppsForms`, so shipping the
--      generator's output verbatim would have called `__mj_BizAppsForms.spCreateRole` — an object
--      that does not exist — on every install. bizapps-common hit the same trap and solved it by
--      hand-writing literal `[__mj]`; the placeholder is better, because it still honours a host
--      that has renamed its core schema.
--   2. Literal `[__mj_BizAppsForms]` -> `[${flyway:defaultSchema}]` on the 20 Forms-schema calls
--      (`spCreateFormStyle`, `spCreateFormCategory`), matching every other migration in this repo.
--
-- No other placeholder appears, and none may: `mj app install` supplies only these two. See the
-- note in mj.config.cjs about the former commonSchema placeholder for what happens when a third
-- one sneaks in. (Written without the `$`-brace form on purpose — `npm run lint:distribution`
-- scans shipped SQL as text and cannot tell a comment from a statement, which is the correct
-- trade: a placeholder mentioned in a comment today is a placeholder pasted into SQL tomorrow.)
--
-- The 5 `spUpdateTemplateParam` calls are the generator's own create-then-update of template params
-- it created moments earlier in this same file. They are not updates to pre-existing state.
-- =============================================================================================

-- Save MJ: Roles (core SP call only)
DECLARE @ID_bee54259 UNIQUEIDENTIFIER,
@Name_bee54259 NVARCHAR(50),
@Description_bee54259 NVARCHAR(MAX),
@DirectoryID_bee54259 NVARCHAR(250),
@SQLName_bee54259 NVARCHAR(250)
SET
  @ID_bee54259 = 'A18E13FC-B2C1-4E77-A3D7-EE775BDE098C'
SET
  @Name_bee54259 = N'Form Respondent'
SET
  @Description_bee54259 = N'Restricted role for anonymous/external respondents who reach a published MJ Form via a scoped magic-link distribution. The ONE deliberate exception to the magic-link read-only convention: it grants CanCreate on Form Responses and Form Response Answers ONLY (no read/update/delete on those), plus read-only on the published form-definition entities the respondent widget must load. It grants nothing else. Authorization is enforced server-side from the session''s mj_scopes union; this role is the entity-permission boundary. Never assign to internal/SSO users.' EXEC [${mjSchema}].spCreateRole @ID = @ID_bee54259,
  @Name = @Name_bee54259,
  @Description = @Description_bee54259,
  @DirectoryID = @DirectoryID_bee54259,
  @DirectoryID_Clear = 1,
  @SQLName = @SQLName_bee54259,
  @SQLName_Clear = 1;

GO

